data: {"id":"gen-1785177601-Q9kE2qjAwTQxa2yYaLnv","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1785177601,"model":"openai/gpt-5.6-terra","provider":"OpenAI","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://doc.traefik.io/traefik/master/reference/routing-configuration/http/load-balancing/service/","title":"Traefik HTTP Services Documentation - Traefik","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"#### Power of Two Choices (p2c)¶\n\nSelects two servers at random and routes the request to the one with the fewest active connections. This algorithm provides better load distribution when servers have varying response times.\n\n[...]\n\n- `wrr`(Weighted Round Robin) - Default strategy, distributes requests evenly across servers in rotation\n- `p2c`(Power of Two Choices) - Selects two random servers and routes to the one with fewer active connections\n- `hrw`(Highest Random Weight) - Uses consistent hashing based on client IP for session affinity\n- `leasttime`- Routes to the server with lowest response time combined with fewest active connections\n\n[...]\n\nP2C Load Balancing -- Using the File Provider\n\n[...]\n\n```\n## Routing configuration\nhttp:\n  services:\n    my-service:\n      loadBalancer:\n        strategy: \"p2c\"\n        servers:\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-1/\"\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-2/\"\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-3/\"\n\n[...]\n\nThe load balancer supports multiple strategies for distributing traffic among servers:\n\n[...]\n\n```\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)"}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}
data: {"id":"gen-1785177601-Q9kE2qjAwTQxa2yYaLnv","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1785177601,"model":"openai/gpt-5.6-terra","provider":"OpenAI","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://doc.traefik.io/traefik/v3.5/reference/routing-configuration/http/load-balancing/service/","title":"Traefik HTTP Services Documentation | Traefik | v3.5","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"## P2C¶\n\nPower of two choices algorithm is a load balancing strategy that selects two servers at random and chooses the one with the least number of active requests.\n\n[...]\n\nP2C Load Balancing -- Using the File Provider\n\n[...]\n\n```\n## Dynamic configuration\nhttp:\n  services:\n    my-service:\n      loadBalancer:\n        strategy: \"p2c\"\n        servers:\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-1/\"\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-2/\"\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-3/\"\n\n[...]\n\n```\n\n[...]\n\nYAML\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\n### Configuration Example¶\n\n[...]\n\nYAML\n\n[...]\n\nTOML\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\n```\n## Dynamic configuration\n[http.services]\n  [http.services.my-service.loadBalancer]\n    strategy = \"p2c\"\n    [[http.services.my-service.loadBalancer.servers]]\n      url = \"http://private-ip-server-1/\"\n    [[http.services.my-service.loadBalancer.servers]]\n      url = \"http://private-ip-server-2/\"       \n    [[http.services.my-service.loadBalancer.servers]]\n      url = \"http://private-ip-server-3/\"\n```"}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}
data: {"id":"gen-1785177601-Q9kE2qjAwTQxa2yYaLnv","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1785177601,"model":"openai/gpt-5.6-terra","provider":"OpenAI","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://doc.traefik.io/traefik/v3.7/reference/routing-configuration/http/load-balancing/service/","title":"Traefik HTTP Services Documentation | Traefik | v3.7","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"#### Power of Two Choices (p2c)¶\n\n[...]\n\nSelects two servers at random and routes the request to the one with the fewest active connections. This algorithm provides better load distribution when servers have varying response times.\n\n[...]\n\nP2C Load Balancing -- Using the File Provider\n\n[...]\n\n```\n## Routing configuration\nhttp:\n  services:\n    my-service:\n      loadBalancer:\n        strategy: \"p2c\"\n        servers:\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-1/\"\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-2/\"\n        - url: \"http://private-ip-server-3/\"\n\n[...]\n\n- `wrr`(Weighted Round Robin) - Default strategy, distributes requests evenly across servers in rotation\n- `p2c`(Power of Two Choices) - Selects two random servers and routes to the one with fewer active connections\n- `hrw`(Highest Random Weight) - Uses consistent hashing based on client IP for session affinity\n- `leasttime`- Routes to the server with lowest response time combined with fewest active connections\n\n[...]\n\n```\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nThe load balancer supports multiple strategies for distributing traffic among servers:\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (TOML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)\n\n[...]\n\nStructured (YAML)"}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}
data: {"id":"gen-1785177601-Q9kE2qjAwTQxa2yYaLnv","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1785177601,"model":"openai/gpt-5.6-terra","provider":"OpenAI","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://docs.nginx.com/nginx/admin-guide/load-balancer/http-load-balancer/","title":"HTTP Load Balancing | NGINX Documentation","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Least Time– For each request, NGINX selects the server with the lowest average latency and the lowest number of active connections. The lowest average latency is calculated based the parameter included with the`least_time` directive. This parameter can be one of the following:\n\n[...]\n\n## Choose a load balancing method\n\nNGINX Open Source supports five load balancing methods: Round Robin, Least Connections, Least Time, IP Hash, and Generic Hash. NGINX Plus supports six load balancing methods: all of the above, plus Random.\n\n[...]\n\n1. - `header`– Time to receive the first byte from the server\n- `last_byte`– Time to receive the full response from the server\n- `last_byte inflight`– Time to receive the full response from the server, taking into account incomplete requests\n2. - `least_conn`– The least number of active connections\n- `least_time=header`(NGINX Plus) – The least average time to receive the response header from the server ($upstream_header_time)\n- `least_time=last_byte`(NGINX Plus) – The least average time to receive the full response from the server ($upstream_response_time)\n\n##\n\n[...]\n\n## Compatibility note\n\n- The least_time load-balancing method is supported in NGINX Plus since Release 6 and in NGINX Open Source since 1.31.0.\n\n## Dynamic configuration using the NGINX Plus API\n\n[...]\n\nNote: When configuring any method other than Round Robin, put the corresponding directive (hash, ip_hash, least_conn, least_time, or random) above the list of`server` directives in the upstream {} block.\n\n[...]\n\nnginx\n\nCopy\n\n```\nupstream backend {\n    least_time header;\n    server backend1.example.com;\n    server backend2.example.com;\n}\n```\n\n[...]\n\nThe Random load balancing method should be used for distributed environments where multiple load balancers are passing requests to the same set of backends. For environments where the load balancer has a full view of all requests, use other load balancing methods.\n\n[...]\n\nWith NGINX Plus, the configuration of an upstream server group can be modified dynamically using the NGINX Plus API. A configuration command can be used to view all servers or a particular server in a group, modify parameter for a particular server, and add or remove servers. For more information and instructions, see Configuring Dynamic Load Balancing with the NGINX Plus API.\n\n[...]\n\nRandom(NGINX Plus only) – Each request will be passed to a randomly selected server. This method takes into account server weights. If the`two` parameter is specified, NGINX first randomly selects two servers, then chooses between these servers using one of the following specified methods:\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\n```\n\n[...]\n\nnginx\n\n[...]\n\nNGINX and NGINX Plus provide Layer 7 (application layer) load balancing for HTTP and HTTPS traffic. You can choose from multiple load-balancing algorithms, ranging from basic Round Robin to more advanced methods such as Least Connections, Least Time, and hashing-based methods. NGINX Plus adds more capabilities such as slow-start, which gracefully reintroduces recovered servers, and session persistence (“sticky” sessions) which routes a client’s requests to the same upstream server.\n\n[...]\n\n```\nupstream backend {\n    random two least_time=last_byte;\n    server backend1.example.com;\n    server backend2.example.com;\n    server backend3.example.com;\n    server backend4.example.com;\n}\n\n[...]\n\nnginx\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\n```\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nnginx\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\n```\n\n[...]\n\nnginx\n\n[...]\n\n```\n\n[...]\n\nnginx\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nCopy\n\n[...]\n\nnginx\n\n[...]\n\nCopy"}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}
data: {"id":"gen-1785177601-Q9kE2qjAwTQxa2yYaLnv","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1785177601,"model":"openai/gpt-5.6-terra","provider":"OpenAI","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"http://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_upstream_module.html","title":"Module ngx_http_upstream_module","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"> Prior to version 1.11.6, incomplete requests were taken into account by default.\n\n> Prior to version 1.31.0, this directive was available only as part of our commercial subscription.\n\n[...]\n\nIf the`header` parameter is specified, time to receive the response header is used. If the`last_byte` parameter is specified, time to receive the full response is used. If the`inflight` parameter is specified (1.11.6), incomplete requests are also taken into account.\n\n[...]\n\nSpecifies that a group should use a load balancing method where a request is passed to the server with the least average response time and least number of active connections, taking into account weights of servers. If there are several such servers, they are tried in turn using a weighted round-robin balancing method.\n\n[...]\n\n> The`least_time` method is available as a part of our commercial subscription.\n\n[...]\n\n| Syntax: | `header`|`last_byte` [`inflight`]; |\n| --- | --- |\n| Default: | — |\n| Context: | `upstream` |\n\n[...]\n\nThis directive appeared in version 1.7.10.\n\n[...]\n\nThe`least_time` method passes a request to a server with the least average response time and least number of active connections. If`least_time=header` is specified, the time to receive the response header is used. If`least_time=last_byte` is specified, the time to receive the full response is used."}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}
data: {"id":"gen-1785177601-Q9kE2qjAwTQxa2yYaLnv","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1785177601,"model":"openai/gpt-5.6-terra","provider":"OpenAI","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/load_balancing.html","title":"Using nginx as HTTP load balancer","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Least-time load balancing in nginx is activated when the least_time directive is used as part of the server group configuration:\n\n[...]\n\n```\n\n    upstream myapp1 {\n        least_time header;\n        server srv1.example.com;\n        server srv2.example.com;\n        server srv3.example.com;\n    }\n\n```\n\n[...]\n\n#### Least time load balancing\n\n[...]\n\nWith the least-time load balancing, nginx selects the server based on which of the following parameters is specified in the least_time directive:\n\n[...]\n\n- `header`— time to receive the first byte from the server,\n- `last_byte`— time to receive the full response from the server,\n- `last_byte inflight`— time to receive the full response from the server, taking into account incomplete requests.\n\n[...]\n\nAnother load balancing discipline is least-time. Least-time selects the server with the lowest average response time and the lowest number of active connections, taking into account inflight requests."}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}
data: {"id":"gen-1785177601-Q9kE2qjAwTQxa2yYaLnv","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1785177601,"model":"openai/gpt-5.6-terra","provider":"OpenAI","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://github.com/nginx/documentation/issues/2026","title":"[Request]: Update upstream-settings docs to reflect least_time LB availability in NGINX OSS","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"> Just confirming — the `least_time` directive was indeed made available in NGINX OSS starting with 1.31.0 (released October 2024). The upstream module changelog for that release lists it as a new feature: https://nginx.org/en/CHANGES. You can verify by checking the `ngx_http_upstream_least_time_module` being built by default in 1.31+. The docs update you're proposing is correct.\n\n[...]\n\nUpdate the upstream-settings documentation to reflect that least_time load balancing is now available in NGINX OSS as of v1.31.0. Previously it was listed as NGINX Plus only.\n\n[...]\n\n**AliMahmoudDev** commented on 2026-06-06T17:26:17Z:\n\n[...]\n\nThe specific file to update is content/ngf/traffic-management/upstream-settings.md. Changes needed:\n\n[...]\n\n- Remove least_time from the Plus-only load balancing methods list\n- Update default LB method for regular upstreams to least_time header\n- Update default LB method for stream upstreams to least_time bytes"}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}
